  "body": "MathJax isn't escaped properly in the log viewer, and MathJax itself puts \\href URLs directly into an attribute. So if your URL closes the attribute with `”>`, you can then inject arbitrary JS e.g. via an SVG <animate> element’s ‘onbegin’ method.\n\nThis could allow agents to modify/hide evidence of behavior in evals.\n\nThe attack chain:\n1. Agent writes a math expression into any transcript field it controls (a message, output, etc.): $\\href{x\"><animate onbegin=eval(atob('…'))…>}{z}$\n1. MathJax renders it to a real SVG <animate> element with an onbegin handler.\n1. The browser fires onbegin automatically → eval(atob(...)) runs the agent's JavaScript in the viewer's origin, with no click or interaction.",
